WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
hello and welcome i'm james murphy and&nbsp;
today we're looking at one of python's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.440
sharpest corners the plus equals operator.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.760
python is widely regarded as one of the&nbsp;
most intuitive programming languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.120
but no matter how intuitive&nbsp;
a programming language is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.040
will always have some features which&nbsp;
are a little bit subtle or confusing.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.600
in python one of those things is the&nbsp;
plus equals operator let's take a look.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.280
in this first example we're trying&nbsp;
to change one of the values of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.280
tuple but tuples are immutable so&nbsp;
this is a pretty obvious error.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.520
in this case we cannot change the&nbsp;
value of tuple of zero because tuple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.080
is immutable note however that it's the&nbsp;
tuple that's immutable, not its elements.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.400
so in this case i have a tuple of pros and&nbsp;
cons which are both lists, you know pros of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.480
subscribing and cons of subscribing, and even&nbsp;
though the tuple is immutable you can of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.400
get out the zeroth element, the&nbsp;
pros, and then add something to it.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.360
that's fine.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.520
you can see if i run it there's no errors.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.320
okay so the tuple itself is immutable but the&nbsp;
zeroth element of the tuple is a list which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.320
is not immutable, so will this syntax allow&nbsp;
me to append another element onto that list?

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.880
when i run the code i get the same error as&nbsp;
before telling me that i can't assign to a tuple.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.440
let's see what happens if we catch that error&nbsp;
with a try except and then print out the list.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.360
well we can see that it definitely hit the&nbsp;
error block but when we print out the list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.040
it also appended the element, what's going on?

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.160
i think most people definitely expect it to either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:40.080
give an error and not append anything or to append&nbsp;
something with no error, so why is it doing both?

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.160
well, sharp corner.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.160
this is, according to the&nbsp;
documentation, a "feature" not a bug.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.200
it has to do with the data&nbsp;
model and how plus equals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.440
is supposed to work on different data types.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.760
in order to get a slightly better understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.680
let's take a look at what plus equals&nbsp;
actually translates to under the hood.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.360
roughly this is what x plus equals y means.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.120
it means create a temporary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.360
call this function iadd for in-place add&nbsp;
and then assign the result back to x.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.320
when the left hand side of the plus equals&nbsp;
is an expression which is not just a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.720
variable name then you can see that this&nbsp;
pattern expands out to something non-trivial.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.600
filling in x sub 0 here shows us&nbsp;
that this actually calls the get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.920
item method of x and similarly putting&nbsp;
it back here calls the set item method.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.800
this is the reason that the list extend&nbsp;
succeeds but the tuple assignment fails.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.880
this first line happily grabs&nbsp;
the zeroth element from the list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.880
and then extends it which is what&nbsp;
the iadd method will do for a list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.000
but when it goes to reassign the result&nbsp;
back to x 0 that's where the error happens.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.600
the exact same thing can happen&nbsp;
with something like x.val&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.480
but instead of calling the get item and set&nbsp;
item it will call the get attr and set attr.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.120
so if you have an immutable object which&nbsp;
contains a mutable object like a list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.360
then you can run into the same kind of trouble.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.520
okay so that's pretty wonky behavior.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.440
why is that a feature and not a bug?

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.520
well actually even just basic&nbsp;
addition of integers depends on it.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:18.080
if i set x to be 1 and then i say x plus equals&nbsp;
1, i expect x to be equal to 2 afterwards.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.680
but in python integers are immutable,&nbsp;
i can't change a 1 into a 2.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.040
i have to point the variable x from&nbsp;
a 1 to a different place to a 2.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.680
you can see this by examining the unique id&nbsp;
that's printed out when i run the function.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.600
initially x's id is whatever&nbsp;
the id is for the built-in one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.120
but afterwards it has to be the&nbsp;
built-in id for whatever two is.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.000
that means that plus equals must have&nbsp;
changed the object that x points to.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.200
this is very different from many&nbsp;
other languages where users have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.640
direct access to built-in&nbsp;
primitive types like integers.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.720
in python there is no division&nbsp;
between built-in primitive types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.240
like integers and floats and all the other types.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.160
all types are treated the same and&nbsp;
therefore must have the same rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.120
but it doesn't stop there it gets even weirder.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.800
look at the behavior of a similar example by&nbsp;
taking x to be an empty list and then appending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.440
one thing onto the end of it using plus equals.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.520
we can see that this time x is pointing to the&nbsp;
same place before and after the plus equals.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.520
for lists which could potentially take&nbsp;
up a lot of memory this makes sense.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.000
if i had a long list i wouldn't want&nbsp;
to copy the whole list over into some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.840
new memory location with one&nbsp;
more element of space on the end.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.040
for list it seems like the id is always preserved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.640
but as we've seen with integers&nbsp;
that doesn't have to be the case.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.760
that can get you into a lot of trouble if&nbsp;
you're not familiar with how python works.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.440
consider this example.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.080
here i'm using a bad list which&nbsp;
is a class that i wrote myself.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.840
i'm not going to show you the details just yet.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.120
bad list inherits from list and all it&nbsp;
does is change the add and iadd methods.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:04.560
what this code tries to do is create an empty&nbsp;
list, print out the list, append something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.560
using plus equals, and then append something by&nbsp;
passing it to a function which calls plus equals.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.240
note here that both times i will be calling&nbsp;
the bad list's implementation of plus equals.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.360
the only difference in this case is that this&nbsp;
plus equals is called inside of another function.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.320
we have the following very surprising results.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.160
of course the list is empty before the append, but&nbsp;
after the append only the one two three is there.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.400
what happened to the four five six?

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.880
we can see that the iadd method was indeed&nbsp;
called twice and yet only one of them succeeded.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:40.800
so what's going on?

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.840
well looking at the implementation&nbsp;
of iadd it's just delegating to add,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.120
which is calling the add method&nbsp;
of the parent list class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.480
so in this case it's actually making a new&nbsp;
list with the contents concatenated together.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.040
that explains why this plus equals&nbsp;
succeeded but this one failed.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.440
here bad gets reassigned&nbsp;
to the newly created list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:09.200
here l (ell) gets reassigned to the newly created&nbsp;
list, but l (ell) was just a local variable.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.360
it's exactly the same thing that would happen&nbsp;
if i was doing this with just integers.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.080
imagine that bad was the integer zero&nbsp;
and here i was doing plus equals.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.160
plus equals 1 would succeed here because it's&nbsp;
in the same scope and i'm reassigning bad, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.760
a plus equals 1 here is just reassigning a local&nbsp;
variable, that wouldn't change the outer scope.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.880
this is extra tricky because, aside from reading&nbsp;
the source code of every class that you use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:40.160
you have no way of knowing whether plus&nbsp;
equals will act this way or this way.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:47.280
in fact the python documentation says that&nbsp;
it's perfectly fine to act this way sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.520
and act this way other times.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.840
thankfully all of the classes that i know of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.640
pick one or the other not both,&nbsp;
but theoretically it is possible.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.360
so if you are writing a class and you&nbsp;
want it to work with plus and plus equals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.320
make sure you do so very very carefully.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.280
i should also mention that the same applies for&nbsp;
any of the other augmented assignment operations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:16.320
so: plus equals, times equals, or equals,&nbsp;
and equals, ... those kind of things.

